{
  "message": "**Question:** How should a sole command-line argument that starts with \"-\" (for example `--help`) be treated: as a file path, or as an option? (FR-001)\n\nWhy it matters: This determines whether the command accepts every single path consistently or reserves some one-argument inputs as command options.\n\n**Recommended:** Option A — Treat it as a file path, consistent with FR-001's contract that exactly one argument is the input path and with a minimal CLI that defines no options.",
  "requestedSchema": {
    "properties": {
      "argument_handling": {
        "type": "string",
        "title": "How to interpret the argument",
        "description": "Choose one, or provide another short answer (<=5 words).",
        "oneOf": [
          {
            "const": "A",
            "title": "File path — treat it as the supplied input path"
          },
          {
            "const": "B",
            "title": "Option — parse it as a command-line option"
          }
        ],
        "default": "A"
      }
    }
  }
}

{
  "message": "**Question:** Must every digit in a timestamp be an ASCII digit `0` through `9`, with other Unicode digits rejected? (FR-005)\n\nWhy it matters: A fixed character set makes timestamp validation predictable and avoids accepting visually similar characters inconsistently.\n\n**Recommended:** Option A — Require ASCII `0`–`9` only, matching the exact machine-readable `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM` format.",
  "requestedSchema": {
    "properties": {
      "timestamp_digits": {
        "type": "string",
        "title": "Allowed timestamp digits",
        "description": "Choose one, or provide another short answer (<=5 words).",
        "oneOf": [
          {
            "const": "A",
            "title": "ASCII only — accept only 0 through 9"
          },
          {
            "const": "B",
            "title": "Unicode digits — accept other decimal digit characters too"
          }
        ],
        "default": "A"
      }
    }
  }
}

{
  "message": "**Question:** What should count as an unreadable input file, including when the supplied path refers to a directory? (FR-002)\n\nWhy it matters: A platform-independent definition lets tests reliably distinguish file-read errors from invalid JSON.\n\n**Recommended:** Option A — Treat a path that is not a regular file (including a directory), or whose contents cannot be fully opened and read as bytes, as unreadable. This supports a stable cross-operating-system directory test while covering actual read failures.",
  "requestedSchema": {
    "properties": {
      "unreadable_definition": {
        "type": "string",
        "title": "Definition of unreadable input",
        "description": "Choose one, or provide another short answer (<=5 words).",
        "oneOf": [
          {
            "const": "A",
            "title": "Not a regular file, or open/read fails"
          },
          {
            "const": "B",
            "title": "Only when the operating system denies reading"
          },
          {
            "const": "C",
            "title": "Only a missing path; others are JSON errors"
          }
        ],
        "default": "A"
      }
    }
  }
}

{
  "message": "**Question:** How should a UTF-8 byte-order mark at the very start of the input file be treated? (FR-003)\n\nWhy it matters: Explicit BOM handling prevents different JSON parsers from accepting the same file differently.\n\n**Recommended:** Option A — Reject the BOM with `error: input must be valid JSON`. This keeps the accepted JSON byte format strict and avoids parser-dependent behavior.",
  "requestedSchema": {
    "properties": {
      "bom_handling": {
        "type": "string",
        "title": "UTF-8 BOM handling",
        "description": "Choose one, or provide another short answer (<=5 words).",
        "oneOf": [
          {
            "const": "A",
            "title": "Reject — report the specified JSON error"
          },
          {
            "const": "B",
            "title": "Ignore — remove BOM and parse the remaining JSON"
          }
        ],
        "default": "A"
      }
    }
  }
}
